The problem

Please describe the technical details of the vulnerability

Summary
Four out-of-bounds reads in the NTFS and exFAT probes of lib/blkid, the library behind the blkid binary AOSP ships as /system/bin/blkid. All four are driven by header fields taken directly from the volume, none of which is validated before use. Two of them copy the out-of-bounds bytes into the volume LABEL that blkid prints.

vold runs this binary on removable media before any fsck and before any mount, in a dedicated u:r:blkid_untrusted:s0 domain. The input is therefore a microSD card or USB-OTG device that an attacker supplies physically.

Affected: external/e2fsprogs lib/blkid/probe.c, AOSP android17-release (3eb1724). The same lines are byte-identical in upstream e2fsprogs master (1.47.4).

Please read the "What I am not claiming" section before assigning severity. I am rating this Low myself.

The four sites
A — probe.c:709 shift with an attacker-controlled exponent → OOB read at probe.c:726

if (ns->cluster_per_mft_record < 0)
        mft_record_size = 1 << (0-ns->cluster_per_mft_record);   /* :709 */
...
buf_mft = get_buffer(probe, off, mft_record_size);               /* :722 */
if (!buf_mft)
        return 1;
if (memcmp(buf_mft, "FILE", 4))                                  /* :726 */
cluster_per_mft_record is a signed byte read from the boot sector. A negative value makes the shift exponent up to 128, which is undefined behaviour; in practice mft_record_size comes out smaller than 4. get_buffer() then allocates exactly that many bytes and the memcmp() at :726 reads 4, past the end of the allocation.

UBSan reports the shift at probe.c:709:23; ASan reports the resulting heap-buffer-overflow READ inside memcmp with probe_ntfs probe.c:726 as frame #2.

B — probe.c:750, attrs_offset used before it is bounds-checked → OOB read at :755

attr_off = blkid_le16(mft->attrs_offset);        /* :750  up to 65535, from the image */
...
while (1) {
        attr = (struct file_attribute *) (buf_mft + attr_off);   /* :754 */
        attr_len  = blkid_le16(attr->len);                       /* :755  <-- reads here */
        attr_type = blkid_le32(attr->type);
        val_off   = blkid_le16(attr->value_offset);
        val_len   = blkid_le32(attr->value_len);

        attr_off += attr_len;

        if ((attr_off > mft_record_size) ||                      /* :762  too late */
            (attr_len == 0))
                break;
The only bounds test on attr_off is at :762, after :755 has already dereferenced the pointer built from it. attrs_offset is a 16-bit field from the volume, so the first read can land up to 64 KiB past the MFT record buffer.

C — probe.c:757, value_offset never validated → OOB read at :774-777, into the label

val_off = blkid_le16(attr->value_offset);        /* :757  from the image, unchecked */
...
if (attr_type == MFT_RECORD_ATTR_VOLUME_NAME) {
        if (val_len > sizeof(label_str))
                val_len = sizeof(label_str)-1;   /* only the LENGTH is clamped */

        for (i=0, cp=label_str; i < val_len; i+=2,cp++) {
                val = ((__u8 *) attr) + val_off + i;   /* :774  offset never clamped */
                *cp = val[0];                          /* :775  read */
                if (val[1])                            /* :776  read */
                        *cp = '?';
        }
val_len is clamped; val_off is not. The bytes read at :775 are written into label_str[129], which probe.c:785-786 publishes as the LABEL tag. This is the one that turns an out-of-bounds read into something a caller can observe.

E — probe.c:1523, exFAT label->length never validated

unsigned char utf8_label[128];
unicode_16le_to_utf8(utf8_label, sizeof(utf8_label),
                     label->name, label->length * 2);   /* :1523 */
label->length is a byte from a 32-byte directory entry. The exFAT specification caps a volume label at 11 characters, but nothing here enforces it, so length * 2 can reach 510 bytes read from a name field that sits near the end of the superblock buffer. The decoded result becomes the LABEL tag at :1524.

