Reachability
attacker-supplied microSD or USB-OTG device
  -> vold, system/vold/Utils.cpp: kBlkidPath = "/system/bin/blkid"
     ForkExecvp(cmd, &output, sBlkidUntrustedContext)   // u:r:blkid_untrusted:s0
  -> blkid_get_dev(cache, path, BLKID_DEV_NORMAL)
  -> blkid_verify()  probe.c:1752
  -> probe_ntfs() / probe_exfat()
This runs before any fsck and before any mount, which is the point of blkid — it is the code that decides what the volume is. No user interaction beyond inserting the card.

What I am not claiming, and one thing you should know
Not claimed: no write primitive, no control-flow influence, no code execution, no crash in a production -O2 build, and no byte of memory that I demonstrated reaching a user. In unsanitized builds these inputs are benign — the probe either fails or emits an in-bounds label. I rate this Low and I do not expect it to clear the functional-PoC bar. I am filing it because the fix has to land in AOSP.

Reproduction hardware: a Motorola Moto G Power 5G (2024), Android 15, patch level 2024-11-01 — not one of the program's supported hardware targets. The finding is in AOSP source, not in device-specific code, and I reproduced it a second time on a completely different architecture and compiler to show it is not device-specific. I am happy to re-reproduce on Cuttlefish or a current Pixel if that is required.

Prior art you should weigh: util-linux maintains a fork of this same libblkid code and fixed the equivalent unvalidated-offset reads in 2022. e2fsprogs did not take those fixes, and AOSP vendors e2fsprogs. So the class is publicly known from the util-linux side, while this specific e2fsprogs/AOSP instance appears unreported — I found no CVE, no AOSP bug, and no Gerrit change for it. I am flagging this rather than letting you discover it.

Not part of this report
Building this library with UBSan also produces eight member access within null pointer reports (tag.c:76, tag.c:114, devname.c:97, save.c:51, save.c:132, cache.c:130, cache.c:137, dev.c:45). These are not bugs. They are the container_of / list_entry offsetof idiom at lib/blkid/list.h:156, which computes an address through a null pointer and never loads from it. The exfat_clean.bin control fires all eight while producing entirely correct output. They should not be patched, and I mention them only so they are not mistaken for part of this report.

Impact analysis

Attacker model
An attacker who can get a storage device into the phone — a microSD card, or anything over USB-OTG. No app installed, no permissions, no user interaction beyond inserting the media. This is the "hostile removable media" model, and blkid is the first Android code to read the volume: vold runs it before fsck and before mount, precisely because nothing about the volume is trusted yet. Android acknowledges that by giving it its own SELinux domain, u:r:blkid_untrusted:s0.

What the four defects give the attacker
All four are reads, at offsets the attacker chooses through unvalidated 8- and 16-bit header fields. There is no write primitive and no influence over control flow.

Two of them (C and E) are more than a memory-safety abstraction, because the bytes they read are not discarded — they are decoded into the volume LABEL that blkid prints and vold consumes:

C, probe.c:774-777: value_offset is unvalidated while value_len is clamped, so the copy loop walks from an attacker-chosen offset into label_str[129].
E, probe.c:1523: label->length * 2 can request up to 510 bytes from a name field near the end of the superblock buffer.
So the shape of the worst case is information disclosure: heap bytes adjacent to the probe buffer surfaced through a user-visible volume label. That is the honest upper bound on impact, and it is why I filed rather than only patching.

Why I am still rating this Low
I did not demonstrate the upper bound, and I will not imply that I did:

In unsanitized -O2 builds the PoCs are benign. Nothing segfaulted in any run. Class A simply fails the probe; class E emits an in-bounds label; I never observed a byte of adjacent heap actually surfacing in output.
The reads are bounded in practice by what the allocator has placed after the probe buffer, which the attacker does not control and I did not attempt to groom.
blkid is a short-lived forked process in a restricted domain with no network access and no persistent state. A read fault would be a failed volume probe, not a device compromise.
There is no write primitive, so this does not chain into anything on its own.
The realistic outcomes are a failed mount of a hostile card, or a small, unreliable leak of adjacent heap into a string the platform then displays. Neither is a user-facing compromise.

Why it is still worth fixing
The defects sit on the one path Android has explicitly designated as untrusted, in code that runs before every other check. They are four missing bounds tests on fields the format already tells you the valid range of. The equivalent fixes already exist in the util-linux fork of this same code and have since 2022; AOSP is carrying the unfixed copy.

The patch is small and local: validate attrs_offset and value_offset against mft_record_size before dereferencing, bound cluster_per_mft_record before the shift, and clamp label->length to the 11 characters exFAT permits.

The cause

Please specify the steps to reproduce the issue, including sample code where appropriate. Please be as detailed as possible.

The reproducer calls blkid exactly the way vold does, so nothing below reaches the library through a path the platform does not use. system/vold/Utils.cpp:662-674 builds:

blkid -c /dev/null -s TYPE -s UUID -s LABEL <path>
and runs it through ForkExecvp(cmd, &output, sBlkidUntrustedContext), i.e. in u:r:blkid_untrusted:s0. vold_mirror.c in the attached archive is that call and nothing else — 18 lines.

Option A — build inside an AOSP tree (recommended; uses AOSP's own libext2_blkid)
The archive ships an Android.bp. Drop the directory into a tree at external/blkid_poc/ and build:

m blkid_probe_poc
adb push $ANDROID_PRODUCT_OUT/system/bin/blkid_probe_poc /data/local/tmp/
adb push *.bin /data/local/tmp/
adb shell 'cd /data/local/tmp && ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=0:detect_leaks=0 \
    UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=0:print_stacktrace=1 ./blkid_probe_poc ntfs_poc.bin'
blkid_probe_poc links libext2_blkid, which is AOSP's own module for external/e2fsprogs/lib/blkid — the same code that ships as /system/bin/blkid. The module is built with ASan+UBSan in recover mode, because the defect is an out-of-bounds READ: without a sanitizer these inputs are benign and nothing visible happens.

For the HWASan variant (arm64 only):

SANITIZE_TARGET=hwaddress m blkid_probe_poc_hwasan
Option B — standalone, no AOSP tree needed
The archive also builds directly from the e2fsprogs sources with the NDK:

./build_and_run.sh          # cross-compile arm64, push to an attached device, run all inputs
./build_and_run.sh host     # build and run on the host instead
./build_hwasan.sh           # HWASan build (needs API >= 34 and -static-libsan)
./symbolize.sh <input.bin>  # run on device and symbolize the frames locally
